-- store_projection_toy.{mm0,mm1} — a *proved* toy model of the memory
-- projection stated in `mm0/store_projection.mm0`.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `store_projection.mm0` states two claims (load and
-- store project onto the ISA model at width 8) and proves neither, because the
-- real proof needs a semantics for `jit_store_i64`/`jit_load_i64` and a
-- simulation between two state types. Those are not in this repo. What *can* be
-- settled today is the shape: pick concrete operations for the two sides and
-- check that the projection then goes through, from a named and cited memory
-- axiom, with nothing assumed about the compiler.
--
-- WHAT IS PROVED HERE (6 theorems, 0 sorry):
--
--   toy_jit_store_visible   a store through the JIT's definition makes the
--                           value visible at the written address
--   toy_isa_store_visible   the same for the model's definition at width 8
--   toy_store_projection    the two definitions agree at the written address
--   toy_load_projection     the two load definitions return the same value
--   toy_jit_store_load_other   a load from a *different* address after a store
--                           reads what a single-cell memory says it should
--   toy_isa_store_load_other   the same for the model's definition at width 8
--
-- The last pair is why the toy memory is one cell rather than opaque: with a
-- single cell, "store then load somewhere else" is the case where the store is
-- *not* visible, and it is the case a projection proof is most likely to get
-- wrong (it is where an implementation that forgot to preserve the other
-- bytes, or that aliased the address, would differ). Here it is stated and
-- proved rather than left unmentioned.
--
-- WHAT IS *NOT* PROVED, and it is the whole distance to W9.3: that the emitted
-- Cranelift instruction computes `jit_store_i64`, that Sail's `vmem_write` at
-- width 8 computes `isa_store_w8`, that an aligned address stays in the linear
-- space, or that the fault branch is never taken there. Here `put`, `get` and
-- `veq` are uninterpreted and the two sides are *defined to be the same term*,
-- so the theorems say "if both sides are this operation, they agree" — which is
-- true, cheap, and exactly as strong as it looks. The real content sits in the
-- axioms below (`at_put`: read-after-write at the written address), and those
-- are cited assumptions, not theorems.
--
-- Keeping this file separate from `store_projection.mm0` is deliberate: that
-- artifact must contain no `axiom` and no `theorem` so it cannot quietly become
-- a proof of something it did not prove, and `scripts/verify-invariants`
-- enforces exactly that on it. Proofs live here, claims live there, and this
-- file cites the claim it models by digest.
--
-- theory coverage: used=put,get,unset,veq,vne unused=none
-- cites: mm0/store_projection.mm0 sha256=2b94039df2faccf5d4be102dfec17e2e72260271b64e6495c15249465ef86df7

provable sort Prop;
delimiter $ ( ) $;

-- ── The toy memory: one cell ──────────────────────────────────────────────
-- `Mem` holds at most one (address, value) pair — a single cell — and `Val` is
-- both address and value, uninterpreted. Nothing here is 64-bit, aligned or
-- linear, so no theorem can claim more than the definitions say; what the
-- single cell buys is that "load from an address that was not written" has a
-- meaning (`unset`) instead of being undecidable, which is what makes the
-- store-then-load-different case provable at all.
sort Val;
sort Mem;
term put (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem;
term get (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val;
-- The empty content of a cell at an address it does not hold.
term unset: Val;
-- Address inequality, uninterpreted like `veq`; the axioms below take it as a
-- hypothesis rather than deciding it.
term vne (x y: Val): Prop;

-- Value equality, as an uninterpreted predicate with its reflexivity axiom.
-- Stating it as a `term` + `axiom` instead of importing an equality theory is
-- what keeps this file's coverage line honest: `veq` is the only equality the
-- proofs use, and it is assumed, not derived.
term veq (x y: Val): Prop;
axiom veq_refl (x: Val): $ veq x x $;

-- The toy's memory semantics, in two axioms — the two cases a one-cell memory
-- has. A write is visible at the address written; at any other address the
-- cell that write replaced is gone, so the read is `unset` rather than
-- whatever was there before. Both are cited assumptions, not theorems.
axiom at_put (m: Mem) (a v: Val): $ veq (get (put m a v) a) v $;
axiom at_put_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (put m a v) b) unset $;

-- ── The two sides of the projection, concretely ──────────────────────────
-- The emitted instruction's effect and the model's effect at width 8, both
-- defined as the same write. In the real projection these are *different*
-- definitions (Cranelift lowering vs. Sail `vmem_write`), and proving them equal
-- is the work this toy skips by construction.
def jit_store_i64 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a v $;
def isa_store_w8 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a v $;
def jit_load_i64 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ get m a $;
def isa_load_w8 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ get m a $;

-- ── What the model proves ────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Note that these are theorems about the *definitions*, not restatements of
-- `at_put`: the goal is written in terms of `jit_store_i64`/`isa_store_w8` and
-- the proofs go through only because the definitions unfold to `put`.
theorem toy_jit_store_visible (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) a) v $;

theorem toy_isa_store_visible (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) a) v $;

theorem toy_store_projection (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) a) (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) a) $;

theorem toy_load_projection (m: Mem) (a: Val):
  $ veq (jit_load_i64 m a) (isa_load_w8 m a) $;

-- Store, then load from an address other than the one written. The hypothesis
-- `vne a b` is the toy's stand-in for the real claim's `aligned8`/linearity
-- side conditions: something about the addresses must be known, and here it is
-- the weakest thing that makes the single cell's answer determinate.
theorem toy_jit_store_load_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) b) unset $;

theorem toy_isa_store_load_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) b) unset $;
